\title{LEWIS: Consistent DAG Selection for Bayesian Causal Discovery}
\description{
LEWIS (Laplace Error Working model for Inference and Selection of DAG)
learns a directed acyclic graph (DAG) from observational data generated
by a linear structural equation model. The errors may be Gaussian for
some variables and non-Gaussian for others. LEWIS uses a Laplace error
working model, a complexity prior on the DAG and a Laplace approximation
of the marginal likelihood. A Metropolis-Hastings sampler draws DAGs,
and the draws are summarized by a restricted partially directed acyclic
graph (RPDAG), a credible ball and posterior probabilities.
}
\details{
The main function is \code{\link[=lewis]{lewis()}}. The building blocks \code{\link[=lewis_schedule]{lewis_schedule()}},
\code{\link[=lewis_score]{lewis_score()}}, \code{\link[=nongaussian_nodes]{nongaussian_nodes()}}, \code{\link[=rpdag]{rpdag()}}, \code{\link[=shd]{shd()}} and
\code{\link[=tl_lingam]{tl_lingam()}} are also exported. The data set \link{tcga_prostate} contains the gene expression data
of the prostate cancer analysis of the paper.
}
\references{
Chaudhuri, A., Bhattacharya, A., Ni, Y. and Sarkar, B. (2026). Consistent
DAG selection for Bayesian Causal Discovery under general error
distributions. Manuscript.
